---
import TutorialLayout from '../../../layouts/TutorialLayout.astro';
import ModelWorkbench from '../../../components/tutorials/ModelWorkbench';
import Math from '../../../components/common/Math.astro';
import MathBlock from '../../../components/common/MathBlock.astro';
---

<TutorialLayout
  title="Van der Pol Oscillator"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Getting Started', href: '/tutorials/getting-started' },
    { label: 'Van der Pol' },
  ]}
  prev={{ label: 'Bouncing Ball', href: '/tutorials/getting-started/bouncing-ball' }}
  next={{ label: 'Series RLC', href: '/tutorials/getting-started/series-rlc' }}
>
  <div class="mb-6 p-4 rounded-lg border border-accent/20 bg-accent/5">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-accent mb-2">What you'll learn</h3>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-sm text-text-muted space-y-1">
      <li>Nonlinear dynamics and the concept of a limit cycle</li>
      <li>How a single parameter can change the character of oscillations</li>
      <li>Why initial conditions don't matter for the steady-state behavior</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    The Van der Pol oscillator is a classic nonlinear system. Unlike the mass-spring-damper, it has a
    <strong class="text-text">limit cycle</strong> &mdash; regardless of where you start, the system
    settles into the same periodic orbit. The parameter <em>&mu;</em> controls the strength of the
    nonlinearity: small values give near-sinusoidal oscillations, while large values produce sharp
    <strong class="text-text">relaxation oscillations</strong> with long plateaus and fast transitions.
  </p>

  <MathBlock tex={`\\begin{aligned}
\\frac{dx}{dt} &= y \\\\
\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\mu(1 - x^2)\\,y - x
\\end{aligned}`} />

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    The nonlinear damping term <Math tex="\mu(1 - x^2)\,y" /> is the key: when <Math tex="|x| < 1" /> it adds energy
    (negative damping), and when <Math tex="|x| > 1" /> it removes energy (positive damping). This push-pull is
    what creates the stable limit cycle.
  </p>

  <ModelWorkbench
    client:only="react"
    initialSource={`model VanDerPol
  Real x(start = 2);
  Real y(start = 0);
  parameter Real mu = 1.0;
equation
  der(x) = y;
  der(y) = mu * (1 - x^2) * y - x;
end VanDerPol;`}
    modelName="VanDerPol"
    tEnd={20}
  />

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-10 mb-3">Things to try</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-text-muted leading-relaxed space-y-2">
    <li>Set <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">mu = 0.1</code> &mdash; the oscillation looks nearly sinusoidal.</li>
    <li>Crank it up to <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">mu = 5.0</code> &mdash; notice the sharp sawtooth shape of relaxation oscillations.</li>
    <li>Change the plot's <strong class="text-text">X axis</strong> from <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">time</code> to <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">x</code> and plot <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">y</code> against it &mdash; this phase portrait reveals the limit cycle as a closed loop, with the trajectory spiraling onto it from wherever it starts.</li>
    <li>Change the initial conditions to <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">x(start = 0.01)</code> and <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">y(start = 0.01)</code> &mdash; the system still converges to the same limit cycle, just takes longer to get there.</li>
    <li>Try <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">x(start = 5)</code> to start outside the limit cycle &mdash; the amplitude shrinks instead of growing.</li>
  </ul>
</TutorialLayout>
